  "figures": [
    {
      "file": "01_before_after.png",
      "title": "Reference dataset: before and after",
      "caption": "All 36 public sequences and all 497 labelled correspondences are included. Revised maps include partial maps: 17 meet the current coverage gates, 17 lack circumference coverage, 0 lack area coverage, and 2 fail. Author data have no defect-class truth; no fruit grades are validated here."
    },
    {
      "file": "02_error_distribution.png",
      "title": "Accuracy and availability must be reported together",
      "caption": "Each curve counts all 497 labels in its denominator, including rejected predictions. The revised method returns 458 predictions: RMS 5.103 px, median 2.264 px, maximum 35.930 px. It predicts 367/497 labels within 5 px. These are development-set results; labels were not used to fit rotations, but the dataset was inspected during development."
    },
    {
      "file": "03_labelled_correspondences.png",
      "title": "Author-labelled feature projections: representative and worst cases",
      "caption": "Yellow: annotated source feature. Green: author target label. Red: predicted target. The first three rows are selected by proximity to each category median error; the last is the largest returned error, so successes are not shown alone. Original image coordinates and author labels are retained in the JSON results."
    },
    {
      "file": "04_map_geometry_control.png",
      "title": "Isolating the map-coordinate defect",
      "caption": "Geometry and rotations are supplied analytically in this control. Repairing the map reference axis and its associated radii fixes the equatorial-rolling failure. This tests downstream mapping independently of texture estimation, tracking and neural models."
    },
    {
      "file": "05_known_defect_areas.png",
      "title": "Known defect areas and grade arithmetic",
      "caption": "A rendered sphere with exact geometry, exact rotations and true longitude-wedge masks is used. Repeated observations of one defect are fused into one map. The predeclared tolerance is one percentage point; all three cases pass. These are synthetic mapping controls, not measurements of the defect model\u2019s accuracy."
    },
    {
      "file": "06_motion_vs_coverage.png",
      "title": "Physical rotation differs from observed map coverage",
      "caption": "The 2022 paper includes approximately 45\u00b0 of pre-roll and post-roll. In an ideal control, 270\u00b0 of physical motion therefore supports 360\u00b0 of longitude. White denotes observed pixels. The 250\u00b0 and 100\u00b0 controls remain ungraded. Production requires 350\u00b0 observed longitude and 65% area; unseen polar caps are not claimed as observed."
    },
    {
      "file": "07_author_map_comparison.png",
      "title": "Supplementary visual comparison with an author map",
      "caption": "The source is a published montage, split into 14 crops without resizing the source pixels. The author map and the independent output use different longitude extents and are displayed at different scales. This is a qualitative comparison, not pixel-level equivalence, SSIM validation, or reproduction of the paper\u2019s classifier."
    },
    {
      "file": "08_actual_model_pipeline.png",
      "title": "The exact user video: saved intermediate stages",
      "caption": "Track 5 is selected as a diagnostic example, not as a representative accuracy sample. The actual D-FINE models ran on CUDA. This run\u2019s deterministic first 32 usable IDs produced 20 partial maps, 12 failures and zero grades. Track 5: 157.6\u00b0 measured motion, 251.9\u00b0 observed longitude. Red overlays are model predictions, not ground truth."
    },
    {
      "file": "09_rejected_trial.png",
      "title": "Producing a complete-looking image is not sufficient validation",
      "caption": "An experimental accumulation of small rotations increased the numerical coverage, but visual inspection showed an apparent repeated landmark in the reconstructed map. This experiment was not enabled in production. Its source snapshot and failed validation evidence remain available. Visual inspection is qualitative and does not replace labelled 3D ground truth."
    },
    {
      "file": "10_full_video_outcomes.png",
      "title": "Complete original video: every reported outcome",
      "caption": "All 515 frames were submitted through the running application API, using the actual detector, tracker and segmenter. The run returned 402 tracked records; these are not manually verified unique fruit counts. 8 received algorithm-assigned grades. The other outcomes are retained. Grade availability is not classification accuracy."
    },
    {
      "file": "11_full_video_graded_example.png",
      "title": "Track 125: first graded ID in the full run",
      "caption": "Selected by smallest graded track ID, not by visual quality. 66 captured views, 33 used; 381.0 degrees measured motion, 360.0 degrees observed longitude, 85.49% observed area, 3.62% predicted defect on observed area: Class 1. This demonstrates complete pipeline execution; the motion, masks and grade have not been independently labelled as correct."
    },
    {
      "file": "12_full_video_coverage.png",
      "title": "Where full-video maps stop before grading",
      "caption": "Every one of the 282 returned maps is plotted, including partial maps. Dashed lines are the 350-degree longitude and 65% area gates. Tracks for which mapping failed have no coverage coordinates and are counted separately in Figure 10. Numerical coverage alone does not establish correct reconstruction."
